   "text": {
    "L1": "Green plants make their own food. This process is called photosynthesis, and it happens mainly in the leaves.",
    "L2": "A common myth says plants get their food from the soil. Soil gives water and minerals, not food.",
    "L3": "A green leaf needs sunlight, carbon dioxide from the air, water from the soil, and chlorophyll.",
    "L4": "Chlorophyll uses sunlight energy to combine carbon dioxide and water. The leaf makes glucose, its food.",
    "L5": "Glucose can be turned into starch and stored. Oxygen is released during photosynthesis.",
    "L6": "A leaf that has made starch turns blue-black when tested with iodine. This shows where food was made.",
    "L7": "Plants also respire all the time, day and night. In sunlight, photosynthesis is faster, so a leaf gives out more oxygen than it uses.",
    "L8": "Now check one pathway: which substance enters the leaf from the air during photosynthesis?"
   },
